# Backend

Use this flow: **controller → use case → domain → repository**.

- Controllers stay thin: parse input, call a use case, map the result to HTTP. No ORM, no business rules.
- Use cases orchestrate domain logic. They must not talk to HTTP, queues, or raw SQL/ORM APIs.
- Domain holds entities, value objects, and domain errors. Keep it free of infrastructure.
- Repositories persist and retrieve only. No business decisions inside them.
- Services must not mix orchestration with infrastructure (DB clients, HTTP clients, brokers).
- Catch infrastructure errors at the adapter boundary and map them to application or domain errors. Do not leak ORM/driver exceptions from use cases or controllers.

## Security and tenancy

- Every mutation writes an audit log (actor, action, timestamp, correlation, tenant).
- Every endpoint requires auth and a permission check unless it is explicitly public.
- Isolate data by tenant whenever the resource is tenant-scoped. Never query without the tenant constraint.

## Tests

- Add integration tests when the change touches DB, API, queue, or an external service.

```text
❌ Controller → ORM / business rules
✅ Controller → UseCase → Domain ← Repository (infra adapter)
```
